## 1. Understanding AdMob

### What is AdMob?

AdMob is Google's ad network for mobile apps. You display ads in your app → advertisers pay Google → Google pays you (after taking a cut).

### How You Get Paid

```
Advertiser pays Google for ad slots
           ↓
Google shows their ad in your app
           ↓
Each view (impression) earns you fractions of a rupee
           ↓
Earnings accumulate in your AdMob account
           ↓
When balance crosses ₹100, Google pays you on the 21st of next month
```

### The 4 Ad Formats You Can Use

| Format | What It Looks Like | When to Show | Earnings (India eCPM) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Banner** | Small rectangle at bottom/top of screen | Always visible while user uses app | ₹25–₹85 per 1000 views |
| **Interstitial** | Full-screen ad covering entire app | Between activities (after MCQ, after experiment) | ₹85–₹250 per 1000 views |
| **Rewarded** | Full-screen video, user watches voluntarily | When user wants a bonus (extra hint, unlock content) | ₹400–₹850 per 1000 completed views |
| **Native** | Ad styled like your normal app content | Inside lists (between chapter cards) | ₹40–₹170 per 1000 views |

> "eCPM" = effective revenue per 1000 impressions. India has lower eCPM than US/EU, but volume can make up for it.

### Key Concept: Ads Only Show in the APK, Not the Web

Capacitor AdMob plugin only works on Android (and iOS). Your `vercel.app` website will still be ad-free — the plugin is a no-op in browsers.

